/* AbiLearn — Social Science MCQ Bank — Economics Ch 2 & Ch 5 (100 Questions) */

// CH21_SOCIAL (Economics Ch2 — Sectors of the Indian Economy)
const SOCIAL_CH21 = [
  { q: 'Which of the following activities belongs to the primary sector?', opts: ['Assembling automobiles in a factory', 'Operating a retail clothing store', 'Running a courier service', 'Catching fish from the sea'], ans: 3, exp: 'The primary sector involves extraction and harvesting of natural resources. Fishing directly exploits a natural resource (the sea), making it a primary sector activity. Manufacturing, retail, and courier services belong to secondary and tertiary sectors respectively.' },
  { q: 'Value added by a firm is best defined as:', opts: ['Value of output minus value of intermediate inputs used', 'Total sales revenue of the firm', 'Wages paid to all employees of the firm', 'Total profit earned after paying taxes'], ans: 0, exp: 'Value added = Value of output − Value of intermediate goods/inputs used. This method avoids double-counting in GDP calculation. Only the additional value created at each stage is counted, not the full price at every stage.' },
  { q: 'Why are intermediate goods excluded from GDP calculation?', opts: ['Intermediate goods are produced by the government', 'Intermediate goods have no market price', 'Intermediate goods are always imported', 'Including them would cause double-counting of value'], ans: 3, exp: 'GDP counts only final goods and services to avoid double counting. If flour is counted when sold to a bakery and again when bread is sold, the flour\'s value gets counted twice. Using the value-added method prevents this distortion.' },
  { q: 'In independent India, which sector has consistently employed the largest share of the workforce?', opts: ['Primary sector', 'Tertiary sector', 'Secondary sector', 'Quaternary sector'], ans: 0, exp: 'Despite contributing a declining share to GDP, the primary sector (agriculture and allied activities) still employs over 40–50% of India\'s workforce, making it the largest employer. This mismatch between GDP share and employment share reflects the underdevelopment of agriculture.' },
  { q: 'Which sector currently contributes the largest share to India\'s GDP?', opts: ['Tertiary sector', 'Primary sector', 'Secondary sector', 'Both primary and secondary equally'], ans: 0, exp: 'The tertiary (services) sector now contributes more than 50% of India\'s GDP, making it the largest contributor. This shift from primary to tertiary dominance mirrors trends in developed economies and reflects growth in banking, IT, trade, and communication.' },
  { q: 'Disguised unemployment is most commonly found in which sector?', opts: ['Agricultural sector', 'Banking and finance', 'Manufacturing industries', 'Transport and communication'], ans: 0, exp: 'Disguised unemployment (hidden unemployment) occurs when more workers are engaged in a task than necessary. In Indian agriculture, a farm requiring 3 workers may have 6 — the extra 3 appear employed but their marginal product is zero. Removing them would not reduce output.' },
  { q: 'If 10 workers on a farm produce 100 kg of wheat but removing 4 workers still yields 100 kg, this is an example of:', opts: ['Seasonal unemployment', 'Cyclical unemployment', 'Structural unemployment', 'Disguised unemployment'], ans: 3, exp: 'When extra workers are employed beyond productive need — and removing them does not reduce output — it is disguised (hidden) unemployment. This is characteristic of subsistence agriculture where family labour overpopulates farms.' },
  { q: 'Under the MGNREGS (2005), the central government guarantees rural workers:', opts: ['50 days of employment per year at minimum wage', '200 days of employment per individual per year', '365 days of employment with permanent job cards', '100 days of employment per household per year'], ans: 3, exp: 'The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGS) 2005 guarantees 100 days of unskilled manual work per household per year to adult members of rural households willing to do public works. It aims to reduce rural poverty and create productive assets.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a feature of the organised sector?', opts: ['Workers can be dismissed without notice', 'There are no fixed working hours', 'Employment is governed by labour laws including EPF and ESI', 'Wages are paid in kind rather than cash'], ans: 2, exp: 'The organised sector is characterised by regulated working conditions, fixed pay scales, and statutory benefits like Employees\' Provident Fund (EPF) and Employees\' State Insurance (ESI). Workers have job security and legal protection, unlike in the unorganised sector.' },
  { q: 'A daily-wage construction labourer who has no written contract, no provident fund, and can be dismissed any day works in the:', opts: ['Organised sector', 'Public sector', 'Cooperative sector', 'Unorganised sector'], ans: 3, exp: 'The unorganised sector consists of small enterprises with fewer than 10 workers, no formal contracts, no EPF/ESI benefits, and irregular wages. Daily-wage labourers in construction, agriculture, and small shops typically belong to this sector.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT a tertiary sector activity?', opts: ['Running a commercial bank', 'Providing telephone services', 'Managing a school', 'Operating a textile mill'], ans: 3, exp: 'A textile mill is a manufacturing activity belonging to the secondary sector. Banking, telephone services, and education are services — they do not produce physical goods — and hence belong to the tertiary (services) sector.' },
  { q: 'The GDP of a country is calculated as:', opts: ['Sum of value added by all productive units in all sectors', 'Total exports minus total imports', 'Total government expenditure in a year', 'Sum of profits earned by all companies'], ans: 0, exp: 'GDP (Gross Domestic Product) is the total monetary value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a year. It equals the sum of value added at each stage of production across primary, secondary, and tertiary sectors.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following pairs correctly matches activity to sector?', opts: ['Weaving cotton cloth — primary sector', 'Baking bread — primary sector', 'Teaching in a school — tertiary sector', 'Extracting coal from mines — secondary sector'], ans: 2, exp: 'Teaching is a service activity with no physical product — it belongs to the tertiary sector. Weaving cloth is manufacturing (secondary), mining coal is primary (natural resource extraction), and baking bread is manufacturing (secondary).' },
  { q: 'The concept of "value added" is used in GDP calculation primarily to:', opts: ['Measure only the profit of producers', 'Include only government production in GDP', 'Measure the depreciation of machinery', 'Prevent double-counting of intermediate products'], ans: 3, exp: 'GDP uses the value-added method to count only the net contribution at each production stage, preventing intermediate goods from being counted multiple times. Only the value added (output minus inputs) at each stage is summed to compute GDP accurately.' },
  { q: 'Historical sequence of sectoral development in most economies moves from:', opts: ['Tertiary → Secondary → Primary', 'Secondary → Primary → Tertiary', 'All sectors develop simultaneously', 'Primary → Secondary → Tertiary'], ans: 3, exp: 'Historically, economies begin with primary activities (agriculture, hunting), then develop secondary activities (manufacturing during industrialisation), and finally tertiary (services) activities grow with rising incomes and urbanisation. India followed this pattern over centuries.' },
  { q: 'Which one of the following statements about the tertiary sector in India is CORRECT?', opts: ['It employs the majority of India\'s workforce', 'Its growth has been slower than the primary sector since 1991', 'It has overtaken the primary sector in its share of GDP', 'It consists mainly of government services only'], ans: 2, exp: 'The tertiary sector has overtaken the primary sector in GDP share and now contributes over 50% to India\'s GDP. However, it does NOT employ the majority of workers — the primary sector still employs more people, reflecting low productivity in agriculture.' },
  { q: 'MGNREGS creates employment primarily by:', opts: ['Setting up factories in rural areas', 'Giving direct cash transfers without work', 'Subsidising private companies to hire rural workers', 'Providing guaranteed public works like road building and canal digging'], ans: 3, exp: 'MGNREGS guarantees employment through public works: building roads, digging canals, water conservation, drought-proofing, and land development. Workers are paid wages (at least minimum wage) for this unskilled manual labour, creating assets alongside income.' },
  { q: 'In the unorganised sector, which of the following is a common form of worker exploitation?', opts: ['Mandatory paid leave', 'Fixed monthly salary', 'Overtime work without extra pay', 'Free medical insurance'], ans: 2, exp: 'Unorganised sector workers are often forced to work overtime without additional wages, have no paid leave, lack job security, and receive no social security benefits. This exploitation occurs because workers have weak bargaining power and labour laws are poorly enforced.' },
  { q: 'The secondary sector is also called the industrial sector because it:', opts: ['Provides services to industries', 'Mines and extracts natural resources', 'Transforms raw materials into manufactured goods', 'Handles trade and distribution of goods'], ans: 2, exp: 'The secondary sector transforms natural/raw materials into manufactured goods using industrial processes. A steel plant converting iron ore into steel, or a factory turning cotton fibre into cloth, are classic secondary sector examples. It adds value through manufacturing and construction.' },
  { q: 'What is the primary reason for the dominance of the unorganised sector in India\'s employment?', opts: ['Government policy restricts entry to the organised sector', 'Most people prefer unorganised work', 'The organised sector has not grown fast enough to absorb the large labour force', 'Unorganised sector pays higher wages'], ans: 2, exp: 'India\'s organised sector has not expanded sufficiently to absorb its massive labour force. Most workers — especially in rural areas, small enterprises, and construction — must accept unorganised employment due to lack of formal job creation, keeping the unorganised sector dominant.' },
  { q: 'Forestry and logging belong to which sector?', opts: ['Secondary sector', 'Tertiary sector', 'Primary sector', 'Quaternary sector'], ans: 2, exp: 'Forestry involves cultivating and harvesting trees — a natural resource activity. It belongs to the primary sector alongside agriculture, fishing, and mining. The products (timber, bamboo, rubber) are then processed by the secondary sector.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is the best example of the government providing services through the tertiary sector?', opts: ['Running a steel plant like SAIL', 'Mining coal through Coal India Limited', 'Manufacturing defence equipment in ordnance factories', 'Operating railway services for passengers and freight'], ans: 3, exp: 'Indian Railways is a government-owned service provider — transporting passengers and goods is a tertiary sector activity. SAIL and Coal India are secondary and primary respectively. Defence manufacturing is secondary. Railways exemplify government in the tertiary/public service sector.' },
  { q: 'The shift of workers from primary to secondary and tertiary sectors in an economy is associated with:', opts: ['Economic decline and falling GDP', 'Increasing agricultural output only', 'Economic development and rising income levels', 'Falling urbanisation'], ans: 2, exp: 'As economies develop, rising incomes shift demand towards manufactured goods and services. Productivity improvements in agriculture release labour to industry and services. This structural transformation — from primary to secondary/tertiary dominance — is a hallmark of economic development.' },
  { q: 'Which statement correctly describes "hidden unemployment"?', opts: ['Workers who are unemployed but not looking for work', 'Workers employed part-time who want full-time work', 'Government employees who are on paid suspension', 'Workers apparently employed but whose removal would not reduce output'], ans: 3, exp: 'Hidden (disguised) unemployment means workers appear employed but are surplus to requirements — their marginal productivity is zero. It is "hidden" because official statistics count them as employed. It is most prevalent in subsistence agriculture where family labour exceeds farm needs.' },
  { q: 'According to NCERT textbook data, the share of workers in the primary sector in 1972–73 was approximately:', opts: ['10%', '27%', '72%', '50%'], ans: 2, exp: 'In 1972–73, approximately 72% of India\'s workforce was engaged in the primary sector (agriculture, fishing, forestry, mining). This proportion has declined over decades as industrialisation and the services sector have expanded, though the primary sector still employs the largest share.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best explains why the government must protect unorganised sector workers?', opts: ['They earn higher wages than organised workers', 'They are all illegal migrants without citizenship', 'The Constitution mandates government employment for everyone', 'They lack bargaining power, job security, and social security benefits'], ans: 3, exp: 'Unorganised sector workers — small farmers, agricultural labourers, domestic workers — are economically vulnerable. They have no trade union power, no written contracts, no EPF/ESI, and can be dismissed arbitrarily. Government intervention through laws, minimum wage enforcement, and social schemes is essential to protect them.' },
  { q: 'Which sector does a software company providing IT services to clients belong to?', opts: ['Primary sector', 'Secondary sector', 'Tertiary sector', 'The sector depends on the size of the company'], ans: 2, exp: 'IT and software services are services — they do not produce tangible goods or extract natural resources. Regardless of company size, software/IT service firms belong to the tertiary (services) sector, which includes banking, transport, communication, trade, and all professional services.' },
  { q: 'The value-added approach to GDP ensures that the contribution of a steel plant is measured as:', opts: ['Total revenue from steel sales', 'Number of workers employed in the plant', 'Price of steel minus price of iron ore and other inputs used', 'Total profit distributed to shareholders'], ans: 2, exp: 'Value added by the steel plant = Value of steel output − Value of iron ore, energy, and other intermediate inputs purchased. This captures the net economic contribution of the plant without double-counting inputs already counted in earlier production stages.' },
  { q: 'Construction of a dam by a private contractor employing daily-wage workers falls in which sector(s)?', opts: ['Primary sector only', 'Tertiary sector', 'Primary and tertiary sector combined', 'Secondary sector only, with workers in the unorganised sector'], ans: 3, exp: 'Dam construction is a manufacturing/construction activity — secondary sector. The daily-wage workers without formal contracts belong to the unorganised sector. So the activity is secondary sector but the employment condition is unorganised. Both aspects are often combined in such large infrastructure projects.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following would INCREASE measured GDP?', opts: ['A housewife cooking food for her family at home', 'A farmer consuming his own crop without selling it', 'A restaurant preparing and selling meals to customers', 'A student helping a friend with homework for free'], ans: 2, exp: 'GDP counts only market transactions. A restaurant selling meals generates market value — it is counted. Unpaid household cooking, subsistence farming for self-consumption, and free tutoring are not market transactions and are not captured in official GDP, though they add to welfare.' },
  { q: 'In which year was the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act enacted?', opts: ['1991', '2000', '2005', '2010'], ans: 2, exp: 'MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act) was enacted by Parliament in 2005. It came into force in February 2006 in 200 most backward districts, and was extended nationwide by 2008. It is the world\'s largest work guarantee programme.' },
  { q: 'The Public Distribution System (PDS) and MGNREGS are examples of the government\'s role in:', opts: ['Expanding the secondary sector', 'Privatising public sector enterprises', 'Reducing the size of the primary sector', 'Providing social protection and employment in the unorganised sector'], ans: 3, exp: 'PDS (subsidised food grains) and MGNREGS (guaranteed employment) are government interventions designed to protect unorganised sector workers and the rural poor. They provide a safety net ensuring food security and minimum income for the most vulnerable workers.' },
  { q: 'If India\'s GDP in agriculture is 15% but agriculture employs 45% of workers, this implies:', opts: ['Agriculture is highly productive', 'Non-agricultural sectors are unproductive', 'Productivity per agricultural worker is low compared to other sectors', 'India\'s GDP calculation excludes services'], ans: 2, exp: 'When a sector employs 45% of workers but produces only 15% of GDP, the average productivity per worker in that sector is far lower than in other sectors. This "productivity gap" between agriculture and industry/services is a central development challenge in India.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following activities would be included in the secondary sector?', opts: ['Banking services', 'Rice cultivation by a farmer', 'Weaving silk sarees in a power loom factory', 'Retail selling of vegetables in a market'], ans: 2, exp: 'Weaving in a power loom factory transforms raw silk threads (a primary sector product) into a finished cloth (manufactured good) — this is secondary sector manufacturing. Banking is tertiary, rice cultivation is primary, and vegetable retail is tertiary (trade).' },
  { q: 'The organised sector offers employees "social security." This refers to:', opts: ['Free housing provided by employers', 'Government grants given to companies', 'Subsidised loans from public banks', 'Benefits like provident fund, gratuity, pension, and medical insurance'], ans: 3, exp: 'Social security in the organised sector includes Employees\' Provident Fund (EPF — retirement savings), Employees\' State Insurance (ESI — health cover), gratuity, maternity benefits, and pension. These protect workers from financial shocks due to illness, old age, or job loss.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is an example of the "tertiary sector supporting the primary and secondary sectors"?', opts: ['A mine extracting iron ore', 'A factory making cars', 'A transport company moving goods from farms to markets', 'A farmer growing cotton'], ans: 2, exp: 'The tertiary sector acts as a support system for primary and secondary sectors. Transport companies move agricultural produce and industrial goods; banks finance farms and factories; communication services coordinate supply chains. Without tertiary services, primary and secondary sectors cannot function efficiently.' },
  { q: 'Over-farming due to excess agricultural labour is linked to which economic concept?', opts: ['Capital deepening', 'Inflation caused by food scarcity', 'Structural surplus in agriculture', 'Diminishing marginal returns and disguised unemployment'], ans: 3, exp: 'When too many workers are added to a fixed land area, each additional worker contributes less and less output (diminishing marginal returns). Beyond a point, adding workers adds zero output — these surplus workers are "disguisedly unemployed." Removing them would not reduce farm output.' },
  { q: 'In post-independence India, which sector was given maximum emphasis in the First and Second Five Year Plans?', opts: ['Services sector', 'Foreign trade and exports', 'Agriculture and heavy industry (primary and secondary)', 'Private banking and finance'], ans: 2, exp: 'The First Five Year Plan (1951–56) prioritised agriculture and irrigation. The Second Plan (1956–61) focused on heavy industry (steel plants, dams). Both plans concentrated on strengthening the primary and secondary sectors as the foundation for economic development.' },
  { q: 'A fisherman who catches fish and sells it in the local market operates in which sector(s)?', opts: ['Only primary sector', 'Only tertiary sector', 'Primary sector (fishing) and tertiary sector (trade/selling)', 'Secondary sector only'], ans: 2, exp: 'The fisherman\'s activity spans two sectors: catching fish is primary (natural resource extraction), while selling it in the market involves trade, which is tertiary. In national accounts, his total value added captures both the primary production and the trading margin.' },
  { q: 'Seasonal unemployment in agriculture differs from disguised unemployment in that:', opts: ['Seasonal unemployment occurs year-round while disguised unemployment is temporary', 'Both types are identical in nature', 'Disguised unemployment occurs in cities while seasonal is only rural', 'Seasonal unemployment means no work during off-seasons; disguised unemployment means surplus workers even during peak season'], ans: 3, exp: 'Seasonal unemployment occurs when workers are idle during agricultural off-seasons (after sowing and before harvest). Disguised unemployment means excess workers are engaged even during the active season — their removal would not reduce output. Both are forms of underemployment but have different causes and timing.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following workers is most likely in the organised sector?', opts: ['A vegetable vendor in a weekly market', 'A domestic helper working in a private household', 'A bank employee working in a nationalised bank', 'A construction labourer on a daily-wage basis'], ans: 2, exp: 'A nationalised bank employee works under formal employment contracts, receives EPF and ESI benefits, has fixed pay scales, and is governed by banking industry labour laws — all hallmarks of the organised sector. The other three are classic unorganised sector workers with no formal benefits.' },
  { q: 'The "service sector" in India includes which of the following?', opts: ['Only government services like administration and defence', 'Only private services like banking and insurance', 'Both government services AND private services including trade, transport, IT, banking, education, and health', 'Only services exported to foreign countries'], ans: 2, exp: 'India\'s tertiary (service) sector is broad, encompassing government services (administration, defence, education, health), private services (banking, insurance, IT, telecom), trade (wholesale and retail), transport, hotels, and real estate. Both government and private services are included.' },
  { q: 'What is the significance of MGNREGS beyond just providing employment?', opts: ['It reduces the government\'s fiscal deficit', 'It trains workers for organised sector jobs', 'It creates durable community assets (roads, wells, check dams) while providing income', 'It increases agricultural imports'], ans: 2, exp: 'MGNREGS has a dual purpose: (1) providing guaranteed income to rural poor during lean periods, and (2) creating permanent community assets like roads, ponds, check dams, and land development works. This combination of social protection and asset creation distinguishes it from mere welfare transfers.' },
  { q: 'Which sector in India has shown the fastest growth since the economic liberalisation of 1991?', opts: ['Primary sector (agriculture)', 'Mining and quarrying', 'Secondary sector (manufacturing)', 'Tertiary sector (services)'], ans: 3, exp: 'Since liberalisation in 1991, India\'s tertiary sector — especially IT/software, banking, telecommunications, and business services — has grown exceptionally fast. India became globally prominent in IT services, making the tertiary sector not just the largest but also the fastest-growing component of GDP.' },
  { q: 'If a baker buys flour for ₹10, uses it to make bread worth ₹25, the baker\'s value added to GDP is:', opts: ['₹25', '₹10', '₹15', '₹35'], ans: 2, exp: 'Value added = Output value − Intermediate input cost = ₹25 − ₹10 = ₹15. Only this ₹15 is added to GDP. The ₹10 was already counted when the flour was produced. Counting ₹25 again would double-count the ₹10 flour value. This is the essence of the value-added approach.' },
  { q: 'The statement "In rural India, a family has 5 members working on a farm that requires only 2 workers" describes:', opts: ['Structural unemployment', 'Open unemployment', 'Disguised unemployment', 'Voluntary unemployment'], ans: 2, exp: 'This is textbook disguised unemployment: 5 workers appear employed on the farm, but only 2 are needed. The other 3 contribute zero marginal output — they are "hidden" unemployed. This is extremely common in Indian subsistence agriculture where land holdings are small.' },
  { q: 'The government\'s role in job creation through public sector enterprises is justified because:', opts: ['Private sector is always inefficient', 'Some essential services (power, railways, water) are unprofitable for private firms but necessary for the public', 'Government enterprises always make profits', 'Labour laws prohibit private sector employment'], ans: 1, exp: 'Governments invest in public sector enterprises where private investment is insufficient — natural monopolies, essential infrastructure (railways, power, telecommunications), and social services (health, education). These sectors may be unprofitable at market prices but are vital for economic development and equity.' },
  { q: 'An increase in agricultural productivity that allows some farm workers to move to manufacturing is called:', opts: ['Green Revolution', 'Industrial recession', 'Import substitution', 'Structural transformation of the economy'], ans: 3, exp: 'Structural transformation refers to the shift of labour and resources from agriculture (primary) to industry (secondary) and services (tertiary) as productivity rises. Agricultural productivity gains release labour (reducing disguised unemployment) that can be absorbed by growing industrial and service sectors.' },
  { q: 'The difference between the public sector and the organised sector is that:', opts: ['They are identical — all public sector workers are organised', 'The private sector is never organised', 'Public sector refers to government ownership; organised sector refers to formal employment conditions — they overlap but are not the same', 'Organised sector includes only MNCs'], ans: 2, exp: 'Public sector = government-owned enterprises (BHEL, Indian Railways, SBI). Organised sector = enterprises with formal employment conditions (EPF, ESI, fixed contracts) — includes both public AND large private firms. Not all organised sector firms are public, and not all public sector workers are in the organised sector (e.g., casual government workers).' },
  { q: 'Which of the following correctly describes the economic logic of GDP calculation using value added?', opts: ['It counts each product once at the final consumer price', 'It counts the raw material separately from the finished good', 'It counts both intermediate and final goods to capture all economic activity', 'It only counts goods exported abroad'], ans: 0, exp: 'The value-added approach effectively counts each product once at the final consumer price, broken into stages. By summing value added (not gross output) at each production stage, we arrive at the final price without double counting. ₹15 baker value + ₹10 miller value = ₹25 bread price.' },
];

// CH22_SOCIAL (Economics Ch5 — Consumer Rights)
const SOCIAL_CH22 = [
  { q: 'The Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) was enacted in India in which year?', opts: ['1975', '1980', '1986', '1991'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Consumer Protection Act (COPRA) was enacted in 1986 to provide better protection to consumer interests. It established a three-tier quasi-judicial redressal machinery and recognised six consumer rights. It was later replaced by the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 with enhanced provisions.' },
  { q: 'Which court handles consumer complaints involving claims between ₹1 crore and ₹10 crore?', opts: ['District Consumer Disputes Redressal Forum', 'State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission', 'National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC)', 'High Court of the concerned state'], ans: 1, exp: 'The three-tier consumer court system: District Forum handles claims up to ₹1 crore; State Commission handles claims from ₹1 crore to ₹10 crore; NCDRC (National Commission) handles claims above ₹10 crore. Appeals flow upward through the tiers.' },
  { q: 'A consumer buys a medicine that turns out to be a duplicate (fake) product. Which consumer right has been violated?', opts: ['Right to choose', 'Right to be heard', 'Right to safety', 'Right to redressal'], ans: 2, exp: 'The right to safety protects consumers from hazardous or defective goods that could harm life or property. A duplicate/counterfeit medicine may contain wrong substances or wrong doses — endangering life. This directly violates the consumer\'s right to safety.' },
  { q: 'The Right to Information (RTI) Act was enacted in India in:', opts: ['1986', '1999', '2005', '2010'], ans: 2, exp: 'The Right to Information Act was enacted in 2005. It empowers citizens to demand information from any public authority within 30 days. For consumers, RTI is linked to the right to information — consumers (and citizens) have the right to know about government decisions, policies, and public services.' },
  { q: 'A shopkeeper sells packaged goods without displaying the MRP (Maximum Retail Price). Which consumer right does this violate?', opts: ['Right to safety', 'Right to information', 'Right to be heard', 'Right to consumer education'], ans: 1, exp: 'The right to information gives consumers the right to know all relevant details about a product: price, quality, ingredients, expiry date, and MRP. Hiding the MRP deprives consumers of the ability to make an informed purchase decision and may lead to overcharging.' },
  { q: 'ISI mark on products is granted by:', opts: ['Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI)', 'Agmark Grading Authority', 'Ministry of Consumer Affairs', 'Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS)'], ans: 3, exp: 'The ISI (Indian Standards Institute) mark, now managed by the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS), certifies that a product meets quality and safety standards. It is mandatory for certain products (electrical goods, cement, LPG cylinders) to carry the ISI mark before being sold.' },
  { q: 'AGMARK is a quality certification primarily applicable to:', opts: ['Electronic consumer goods', 'Pharmaceutical medicines', 'Agricultural and allied products like ghee, edible oils, and cereals', 'Gold and silver jewellery'], ans: 2, exp: 'AGMARK (Agricultural Marking) is a certification mark for processed agricultural and food products — ghee, honey, edible oils, cereals, pulses. It ensures the product meets prescribed quality standards. It is administered by the Directorate of Marketing and Inspection under the Ministry of Agriculture.' },
  { q: 'A television advertisement claims a fairness cream will make skin white in 7 days, which is scientifically impossible. This is an example of:', opts: ['Unfair trade practice through misleading advertisement', 'Consumer education by the manufacturer', 'A valid exercise of right to choose', 'Product safety violation only'], ans: 0, exp: 'A misleading advertisement makes false or exaggerated claims about a product. Such ads constitute unfair trade practice under COPRA. Consumers who buy based on false claims have the right to seek redressal. Misleading ads also violate the right to information.' },
  { q: 'The Hallmark symbol on jewellery certifies:', opts: ['The jewellery has been exported', 'The jewellery is insured against theft', 'The purity of gold or silver in the jewellery as per BIS standards', 'The seller has paid all taxes'], ans: 2, exp: 'Hallmark is the BIS (Bureau of Indian Standards) certification for gold and silver jewellery, indicating the purity (e.g., 916 Hallmark = 22 carat gold = 91.6% purity). It protects consumers from being sold adulterated or lower-purity gold/silver at higher prices.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT one of the six consumer rights recognised under COPRA?', opts: ['Right to safety', 'Right to choose', 'Right to redressal', 'Right to employment'], ans: 3, exp: 'The six consumer rights under COPRA are: (1) Right to Safety, (2) Right to Information, (3) Right to Choose, (4) Right to be Heard, (5) Right to Redressal, (6) Right to Consumer Education. "Right to employment" is not among them — it is a fundamental economic right, not a consumer right.' },
  { q: 'The consumer movement in India gained momentum in the 1960s primarily due to:', opts: ['High taxation on consumer goods', 'Failure of the public distribution system', 'Rise of multinational corporations exploiting consumers', 'Widespread food adulteration and shortages during that period'], ans: 3, exp: 'India\'s consumer movement emerged strongly in the 1960s — a period of food scarcity and widespread adulteration of essential commodities like milk, ghee, edible oils, and pulses. Consumers were cheated on quality and quantity, triggering organised consumer activism and ultimately leading to COPRA 1986.' },
  { q: 'A consumer files a case in the District Consumer Forum claiming ₹75 lakh in damages. Is this allowed?', opts: ['No, such cases must go directly to the High Court', 'No, ₹75 lakh exceeds the District Forum\'s limit of ₹20 lakh', 'Yes, but only if the consumer has a lawyer', 'Yes, ₹75 lakh is within the District Forum\'s revised jurisdiction of up to ₹1 crore'], ans: 3, exp: 'Under the revised consumer protection framework, the District Consumer Disputes Redressal Forum handles claims up to ₹1 crore. ₹75 lakh is within this limit, so the consumer can file at the District Forum. (Previously it was ₹20 lakh, but revised under the 2019 Act.)' },
  { q: 'The right to be heard means consumers have the right to:', opts: ['Sue manufacturers in criminal courts', 'Have their complaints considered at consumer forums and in policy-making', 'Demand free replacement for all products', 'Demand refunds without any formal process'], ans: 1, exp: 'The right to be heard means consumers\' interests and complaints must be given fair consideration in forums, tribunals, and government policy decisions. Consumer organisations must have representation in consumer protection committees. It ensures consumers are not dismissed or ignored when they raise grievances.' },
  { q: 'A consumer buys packaged dal that weighs only 900g instead of the labelled 1 kg. This is an example of:', opts: ['Adulteration', 'Overpricing', 'Deficiency of service', 'Underweight/short measurement — an unfair trade practice'], ans: 3, exp: 'Selling goods below the declared/labelled quantity is "short measurement" or "underweight" — a common form of consumer exploitation and unfair trade practice. This violates the right to information (false labelling) and right to safety (economic harm). The consumer can file a complaint under COPRA.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following best describes "adulteration" as a consumer rights issue?', opts: ['Selling goods past their expiry date', 'Charging more than the MRP', 'Using misleading brand names', 'Adding inferior or harmful substances to food products to increase volume or profit'], ans: 3, exp: 'Adulteration means deliberately mixing inferior, cheaper, or harmful substances into food or other products — e.g., adding chalk to milk, mixing sand with sugar, using artificial colours in spices. It cheats consumers on quality and can cause serious health harm, violating both right to safety and right to information.' },
  { q: 'Public Interest Litigation (PIL) is relevant to consumer rights because:', opts: ['Any citizen can file a PIL in court on behalf of consumers or the public on systemic consumer rights issues', 'PILs guarantee compensation to individual consumers automatically', 'PILs bypass the consumer court system entirely', 'Only consumer organisations can file PILs'], ans: 0, exp: 'PIL allows any citizen or consumer organisation to approach High Courts or the Supreme Court on systemic consumer exploitation issues — e.g., contaminated water supply, hazardous products flooding the market. It is a powerful tool for mass consumer protection beyond individual complaints in consumer courts.' },
  { q: 'Under COPRA, the three-tier consumer dispute redressal system consists of:', opts: ['Taluk Court, District Court, High Court', 'Consumer Council, Consumer Tribunal, Supreme Consumer Court', 'Consumer Ombudsman, State Ombudsman, National Ombudsman', 'District Consumer Forum, State Consumer Commission, National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission'], ans: 3, exp: 'COPRA established: (1) District Consumer Disputes Redressal Forum (district level), (2) State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (state level), (3) National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission — NCDRC (national level, New Delhi). Each tier handles different claim amounts, with appeals going to the next tier.' },
  { q: 'The right to consumer education means:', opts: ['The government must provide free school education to all consumers', 'Consumer organisations must publish newspapers', 'Companies must train consumers to use their products', 'Consumers have the right to acquire knowledge and skills to make informed purchasing decisions'], ans: 3, exp: 'The right to consumer education means consumers have the right to acquire knowledge about consumer rights, fair trade practices, product quality standards, and how to seek redressal. Consumer education is fundamental — without awareness, other rights cannot be exercised effectively.' },
  { q: 'A pharmaceutical company sells an expired drug without changing the expiry date label. Under which category does this fall?', opts: ['Misleading advertisement only', 'Deficiency of service', 'Unfair and deceptive trade practice violating right to safety and right to information', 'Breach of contract only'], ans: 2, exp: 'Selling an expired drug with a falsified/unchanged expiry date involves: (1) deceptive trade practice (concealing material information — the expiry date), (2) violation of right to safety (expired drugs may be harmful or ineffective), and (3) violation of right to information (false labelling). It is both criminally and civilly actionable under COPRA and drug laws.' },
  { q: 'If a consumer wins a case in the District Consumer Forum, the opposite party can appeal to:', opts: ['The Supreme Court directly', 'The High Court of the state', 'The National Consumer Commission directly', 'The State Consumer Commission within 30 days'], ans: 3, exp: 'Appeals from the District Consumer Forum go to the State Consumer Commission (not the High Court or Supreme Court directly), within 30 days of the order. From the State Commission, appeals go to NCDRC; from NCDRC, to the Supreme Court. This hierarchical system ensures accessible and affordable consumer justice.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following scenarios represents a violation of the "right to choose"?', opts: ['A consumer voluntarily chooses the most expensive product', 'A consumer picks a product without reading the label', 'A cable TV operator forces consumers to pay for bundled channels they don\'t want', 'A shop offers only one brand of a product'], ans: 2, exp: 'The right to choose guarantees consumers access to a variety of goods at competitive prices without coercion. Forcing consumers to buy unwanted bundled channels (tying arrangement) denies free choice. A shop stocking one brand is not inherently violating this right, but monopolistic compulsion is.' },
  { q: 'The consumer movement is described as a "social force" because:', opts: ['It is backed by the government only', 'It is funded by large corporations', 'It focuses only on price regulation', 'It emerged from collective consumer action against exploitation, eventually securing legal protection through COPRA'], ans: 3, exp: 'The consumer movement arose organically from civil society — consumers, activists, and organisations collectively fighting adulteration, overcharging, and malpractice. This social pressure over decades (1960s–1980s) forced the government to enact COPRA 1986, demonstrating how social force can achieve legal reform.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is a responsibility of consumers, not just a right?', opts: ['Being aware of rights and not being misled by false advertisements', 'Demanding the cheapest possible goods always', 'Expecting all consumer problems to be solved by the government', 'Avoiding all branded goods'], ans: 0, exp: 'Consumer protection requires active consumer participation: being informed, checking labels, reporting malpractices, and not being deceived by misleading ads. Consumer education and awareness are both rights AND responsibilities. Passive consumers who do not exercise their rights undermine the consumer protection framework.' },
  { q: 'A hospital charges a patient for medicines and tests that were never administered. This is:', opts: ['Permissible as hospitals can set their own prices', 'Only a civil dispute outside COPRA', 'A criminal matter beyond consumer courts\' jurisdiction', 'Deficiency of service and unfair trade practice under COPRA'], ans: 3, exp: 'Charging for services not rendered is "deficiency of service" — a ground for complaint under COPRA. Consumer courts have jurisdiction over medical services. The patient can file a complaint at the District Consumer Forum seeking refund of the wrongly charged amount plus compensation.' },
  { q: 'The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC) is located in:', opts: ['Mumbai', 'Chennai', 'New Delhi', 'Kolkata'], ans: 2, exp: 'The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC) is the apex consumer court under COPRA, located in New Delhi. It handles complaints involving claims above ₹10 crore (revised under 2019 Act) and hears appeals from State Consumer Commissions.' },
  { q: 'A consumer purchases a washing machine that catches fire due to a manufacturing defect. The consumer can file a complaint on grounds of:', opts: ['Deficiency of service and violation of right to be heard', 'Violation of right to choose only', 'Misleading advertisement only', 'Product liability — defective goods violating right to safety'], ans: 3, exp: 'A manufacturing defect causing fire is a product liability issue — the manufacturer has placed a hazardous product in the market, violating the consumer\'s right to safety. The consumer can claim compensation for damage to property, medical expenses (if injured), and mental agony under COPRA.' },
  { q: 'Why is consumer awareness described as the "first step" in consumer protection?', opts: ['Consumer courts cannot function without consumer complaints', 'Consumer organisations need consumer fees to operate', 'The government relies on consumers to monitor markets', 'An unaware consumer cannot exercise rights, making all legal provisions ineffective'], ans: 3, exp: 'Legal rights are meaningless if consumers do not know they exist. An unaware consumer will not file complaints, will be cheated repeatedly, and cannot pressure markets toward fairness. Consumer education and awareness are foundational — without them, COPRA and consumer courts cannot fulfil their purpose.' },
  { q: 'The slogan "Jago Grahak Jago" (Wake Up Consumer) is associated with:', opts: ['The Indian Chamber of Commerce', 'The Bureau of Indian Standards', 'The UN Consumer Rights Day', 'The Government of India\'s consumer awareness campaign'], ans: 3, exp: '"Jago Grahak Jago" is the Government of India\'s consumer awareness campaign to educate consumers about their rights, how to identify genuine products (ISI, AGMARK, Hallmark), how to check MRP, and how to file complaints. It is run by the Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is NOT a valid ground for filing a consumer complaint under COPRA?', opts: ['Defective goods', 'Deficiency of service', 'Unfair trade practices', 'Unhappiness with the price of a legally sold product at MRP'], ans: 3, exp: 'COPRA allows complaints for defective goods, deficiency of service, unfair trade practices (misleading ads, short measurement), and overcharging above MRP. A consumer cannot complain merely because they are unhappy with the MRP — the law does not guarantee the cheapest price, only fair pricing and honest dealing.' },
  { q: 'BIS (Bureau of Indian Standards) certification is important for consumers because:', opts: ['It guarantees the lowest market price', 'It provides insurance for product damage', 'It certifies products meet safety and quality standards, reducing risk of buying hazardous/substandard goods', 'It allows consumers to return products within one year'], ans: 2, exp: 'BIS certification (ISI mark, Hallmark) assures consumers that a product has been independently tested and meets prescribed safety, quality, and performance standards. For products like electrical appliances, helmets, and LPG cylinders, BIS certification is mandatory — substandard products can cause fires, accidents, or health hazards.' },
  { q: 'Which international body\'s guidelines for consumer protection influenced India\'s COPRA 1986?', opts: ['World Trade Organisation (WTO)', 'International Monetary Fund (IMF)', 'World Health Organisation (WHO)', 'United Nations guidelines on Consumer Protection (1985)'], ans: 3, exp: 'The United Nations issued Consumer Protection Guidelines in 1985, recognising consumer rights globally and urging member states to enact consumer protection laws. India\'s COPRA 1986 was strongly influenced by these UN guidelines, which are also the basis for World Consumer Rights Day observed on March 15.' },
  { q: 'A consumer complaint filed in the District Consumer Forum about a defective refrigerator takes three years to resolve. Is this acceptable under COPRA?', opts: ['Yes, consumer cases routinely take years like civil court cases', 'Yes, if the company requests delays they must be granted', 'No, COPRA intends for cases to be resolved within 3–5 months to provide speedy justice', 'No, the consumer loses the right to complain after 2 years'], ans: 2, exp: 'COPRA aims to provide speedy, simple, and inexpensive justice. Consumer courts are supposed to resolve cases within 3–5 months (not years). Delays defeat the purpose. Despite this intent, actual backlogs cause delays, which is why consumer court reform and additional benches are advocated.' },
  { q: 'Overcharging above MRP by a retailer violates which consumer right specifically?', opts: ['Right to safety', 'Right to consumer education', 'Right to information and right to choose (at fair price)', 'Right to be heard'], ans: 2, exp: 'The MRP (Maximum Retail Price) is the highest price at which a product can be sold. Charging above MRP violates the right to information (the MRP itself is information — it informs the consumer of the legal maximum) and right to choose (consumer chose the product based on its stated price). It is also an unfair trade practice.' },
  { q: 'World Consumer Rights Day is observed on:', opts: ['January 30', 'October 2', 'November 14', 'March 15'], ans: 3, exp: 'World Consumer Rights Day is observed on March 15 every year, marking the date in 1962 when US President John F. Kennedy first articulated consumer rights in a speech to Congress. It is used globally to promote consumer awareness, with theme-based campaigns each year.' },
  { q: 'A consumer bought a branded shampoo that caused severe hair loss. The MOST appropriate consumer right to invoke is:', opts: ['Right to choose', 'Right to be heard', 'Right to safety', 'Right to consumer education'], ans: 2, exp: 'The right to safety protects consumers against goods that are hazardous to life and property. A shampoo causing hair loss due to defective formulation directly violates this right. The consumer can file a complaint seeking product recall, compensation for damage, and penalty on the manufacturer.' },
  { q: 'In India, consumer courts are different from regular civil courts in that:', opts: ['Consumer courts can award death penalties', 'Consumer courts can only hear cases involving the government', 'Consumer courts are run by private companies', 'Consumer courts are simpler, cheaper (nominal fee), and faster — specifically designed for consumer disputes without needing lawyers'], ans: 3, exp: 'Consumer courts under COPRA are designed to be accessible: filing fees are nominal, cases can be argued without a lawyer, procedures are simpler than civil courts, and resolution is supposed to be faster. This makes justice accessible even to small consumers with low-value complaints.' },
  { q: 'A consumer sees "100% natural, no chemicals" on a product label. If this claim is false, it is:', opts: ['An acceptable marketing practice', 'A violation of the right to redressal only', 'Not covered under COPRA as it involves labelling, not a service', 'A misleading advertisement violating right to information'], ans: 3, exp: 'False product label claims ("100% natural," "clinically proven," "zero fat") are misleading advertisements. They violate the consumer\'s right to information by providing false material facts that influence purchase decisions. COPRA covers both advertisements and product labels as part of consumer protection.' },
  { q: 'The three-tier consumer court system under COPRA is described as "quasi-judicial" because:', opts: ['It only makes recommendations, not binding orders', 'It has powers similar to civil courts (summon witnesses, take evidence, award compensation) but is not part of the regular court system', 'It is run by consumer organisations, not the government', 'Its decisions can never be appealed'], ans: 1, exp: 'Consumer courts are quasi-judicial bodies: they follow court-like procedures (evidence, hearings, cross-examination), can summon witnesses, and pass binding orders — but they are not regular courts under the Civil Procedure Code. They are specialised tribunals with simpler procedures for faster, affordable justice.' },
  { q: 'A farmer sells adulterated milk by adding water and urea. This violates consumers\' right to:', opts: ['Right to redressal only', 'Right to choose and right to be heard', 'Right to safety and right to information', 'Right to consumer education only'], ans: 2, exp: 'Milk adulteration (adding water reduces nutrition; adding urea is toxic) violates: (1) right to safety — urea in milk is a health hazard; (2) right to information — consumers are not told the milk is adulterated. Adulteration is both a consumer rights violation and a criminal offence under food safety laws.' },
  { q: 'The role of consumer organisations in India includes:', opts: ['Manufacturing products for consumers', 'Setting the MRP for all products', 'Running consumer courts on behalf of the government', 'Educating consumers, testing products, filing PILs, and pressuring government for better laws'], ans: 3, exp: 'Consumer organisations like Consumer Guidance Society of India, VOICE, and Consumer India: educate consumers about rights; conduct comparative product testing; file PILs and cases on behalf of consumers; lobby government for stronger consumer laws; and publish consumer guides/magazines. They are the backbone of the consumer movement.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following would give a consumer the STRONGEST grounds for redressal under COPRA?', opts: ['A consumer dislikes the taste of legally sold food', 'A consumer finds a competitor\'s product cheaper after purchase', 'A consumer regrets a voluntary purchase decision', 'A consumer buys a helmet without BIS certification that shatters in a minor accident'], ans: 3, exp: 'A helmet without BIS certification that fails in an accident is a clear case of: defective goods + violation of mandatory safety standards + violation of right to safety. The consumer has strong grounds for compensation. Taste preferences, price comparisons post-purchase, and voluntary decisions are not grounds under COPRA.' },
  { q: 'What is the relationship between RTI Act 2005 and consumer rights?', opts: ['RTI is unrelated to consumer rights', 'RTI replaces COPRA for government service complaints', 'RTI empowers consumers (as citizens) to demand information from public authorities about services, pricing, and regulatory actions that affect consumers', 'RTI only applies to election-related information'], ans: 2, exp: 'RTI Act 2005 is a powerful tool for consumers: they can demand information from government departments about PDS pricing, water quality testing, drug approvals, or regulatory action against companies. It reinforces the consumer right to information specifically for government services and public authority actions.' },
  { q: 'A consumer successfully wins a case in the State Consumer Commission. The manufacturer appeals to NCDRC. The consumer then appeals NCDRC\'s decision to:', opts: ['The State High Court', 'Back to the District Forum', 'The Ministry of Consumer Affairs', 'The Supreme Court of India'], ans: 3, exp: 'The appeal hierarchy in consumer courts: District Forum → State Commission → NCDRC → Supreme Court of India. If either party is aggrieved by NCDRC\'s decision, the final appeal lies with the Supreme Court of India. This ensures constitutional oversight of consumer justice.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following is an example of "deficiency of service" under COPRA?', opts: ['A manufacturer selling a product at a loss', 'A bank charging legitimate interest on loans', 'An insurance company refusing to pay a valid claim without reason', 'A shop being closed on a public holiday'], ans: 2, exp: 'Deficiency of service means failure to perform the agreed service with due care and competence. An insurance company unreasonably rejecting a valid claim is a classic deficiency of service — the company collected premium (entered a service agreement) but failed to honour its commitment when required.' },
  { q: 'Consumer exploitation through "duplicate medicines" poses which unique danger compared to other consumer frauds?', opts: ['Only financial loss, no physical harm', 'It only affects elderly consumers', 'It is only relevant for over-the-counter medicines', 'Direct risk to life — fake medicines may contain no active ingredient or wrong substances, causing treatment failure or poisoning'], ans: 3, exp: 'Duplicate medicines are among the most dangerous consumer frauds: they may contain no therapeutic ingredient (causing disease to progress), wrong ingredients (causing adverse reactions), or contaminants (causing poisoning). Unlike a substandard appliance, a fake medicine can directly cause death or permanent disability.' },
  { q: 'The phrase "consumer is the king" in a free market ideally means:', opts: ['Consumers legally own all market enterprises', 'Government never needs to intervene in markets', 'Consumer demand drives production decisions — firms must satisfy consumer preferences to survive', 'Consumers can demand any price they wish'], ans: 2, exp: '"Consumer is the king" reflects the market logic that firms must produce what consumers want at prices they accept or face losses and exit. In theory, consumer sovereignty drives market outcomes. In reality, market failures (monopolies, information asymmetry, adulteration) mean government intervention through COPRA is essential.' },
  { q: 'A misleading advertisement for a children\'s cereal claims it provides "100% daily nutrition" but it contains only 10% of required nutrients. Under COPRA, this primarily violates:', opts: ['Right to safety and right to information', 'Right to choose and right to consumer education', 'Right to be heard only', 'Right to redressal only'], ans: 0, exp: 'A false nutritional claim violates: (1) right to information — the label/ad misrepresents material facts about nutrition content; (2) right to safety — malnourished children relying on this product face health harm. Parents can file a complaint against the company for both defective goods and misleading advertising.' },
  { q: 'Which of the following BEST distinguishes COPRA from ordinary civil law for consumer disputes?', opts: ['COPRA awards only money compensation; civil law can award injunctions', 'COPRA applies only to goods, not services', 'Civil courts cannot hear consumer cases at all after COPRA', 'COPRA provides a specialised, faster, cheaper, and simpler forum specifically for consumer grievances without mandatory legal representation'], ans: 3, exp: 'COPRA\'s key distinction from regular civil courts: (1) specialised consumer focus, (2) simple filing procedures with nominal fees, (3) no mandatory lawyer requirement, (4) faster resolution (3–5 months intended), (5) accessible to even small-value complaints. Regular civil courts are expensive, slow, and procedurally complex for small consumer disputes.' },
  { q: 'A consumer organisation tests and finds that a popular "100% pure honey" brand is mixed with sugar syrup. The most effective legal action is:', opts: ['Write a letter to the company only', 'Stop buying the product and inform friends', 'File a PIL in High Court on behalf of all consumers affected by the systemic fraud', 'File an individual consumer complaint at District Forum'], ans: 2, exp: 'When consumer fraud is systemic (affecting millions of honey buyers nationwide), a PIL (Public Interest Litigation) filed by a consumer organisation in the High Court is most effective — it seeks product recall, penalty, and corrective action on behalf of all consumers at once. Individual complaints address only single cases; PIL addresses the structural problem.' },
  { q: 'Under the three-tier consumer court system, if a consumer\'s complaint is about ₹50 lakh insurance claim denial, where should it be filed?', opts: ['NCDRC directly as insurance is a financial matter', 'District Consumer Forum as ₹50 lakh is within its ₹1 crore limit', 'State Consumer Commission as insurance cases are reserved for state level', 'Civil court as COPRA doesn\'t cover insurance'], ans: 1, exp: '₹50 lakh is below ₹1 crore, so the District Consumer Forum has jurisdiction. Insurance services are covered under COPRA — denial of valid insurance claim is "deficiency of service." The consumer files at the District Forum; if dissatisfied with the outcome, can appeal to the State Commission within 30 days.' },
];
